Stop rescanning when searching back for a page, and limit false pages per open.

Our own patch (AvP). Upstream's main has the same code. A file of one short link
followed by false pages, each of which looks like a page and fails its checksum, was
very slow to open: 4 MB of them took 129 seconds, and the time grew with the square of
their size.

Searching back for the last page steps back 64 KB at a time, and each pass went on to
the end of what it was searching, over everything the passes before it had already
searched. A pass now stops where the last one started, until it finds a page. From
there it goes on to the end as before, so the search finds the same pages.

That leaves one checksum each time a false page is met, about 1 second per MB. So an
open may meet false pages at most 1,024 times, over the whole file. A search that passes
one twice counts it twice, so fewer than 1,024 of them can be enough to refuse a file.
Real files have none. Reading audio after the file is open is not limited.

--- a/lib/codec_internal.h
+++ b/lib/codec_internal.h
@@ -90,6 +90,13 @@ typedef struct private_state {
 *********************************************************************/
 
 #include "highlevel.h"
+/* The most times one open may meet a false page, over the whole file. A false
+   page looks like a page but fails its checksum. A search that passes one twice
+   counts it twice, so fewer false pages than this can be enough to refuse a
+   file. Real files have none: 0 in 30,458 pages of 289 files measured. See
+   _too_many_false_pages in vorbisfile.c. */
+#define VORBIS_MAX_FALSE_PAGES 1024
+
 typedef struct codec_setup_info {
 
   /* Vorbis supports only short and long blocks, but allows the
@@ -129,6 +136,10 @@ typedef struct codec_setup_info {
   int         halfrate_flag; /* painless downsample for decode */
   long        book_memory_left; /* what this file may still ask for, in bytes:
                                    see VORBIS_BOOK_MEMORY_LIMIT */
+  long        false_pages_left; /* how many more times this open may meet a
+                                   false page:
+                                   see VORBIS_MAX_FALSE_PAGES. -1 means the file
+                                   is open, so they are no longer counted */
 } codec_setup_info;
 
 extern vorbis_look_psy_global *_vp_global_look(vorbis_info *vi);
